1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,075
When an AI coding agent finishes a task and reports success, what

2
00:00:04,075 --> 00:00:08,151
has actually been verified? The sources here describe workflows where success can

3
00:00:08,151 --> 00:00:12,226
be reported without a separate result check. Different verification tools answer different

4
00:00:12,226 --> 00:00:16,302
questions, and treating any single one as sufficient is how silent failures

5
00:00:16,302 --> 00:00:18,000
slip through looking like completions.

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,262
Start with something you probably trust without thinking: a green exit code.

7
00:00:22,262 --> 00:00:26,525
When a compiler exits zero, it has observed real work—source files went

8
00:00:26,525 --> 00:00:30,787
in, object files came out. A Stack Overflow contributor describes an agent

9
00:00:30,787 --> 00:00:35,049
loop that turns the model’s completion signal into exit zero without checking

10
00:00:35,049 --> 00:00:39,311
the result. In that design, nothing independently verifies the work. Other workflows

11
00:00:39,311 --> 00:00:43,574
can add those checks. That status is a self-assessment dressed as an

12
00:00:43,574 --> 00:00:47,836
observation, and every downstream consumer—your CI dashboard, your retry logic, your team

13
00:00:47,836 --> 00:00:52,098
lead glancing at a green run—receives a claim, not evidence. Here is

14
00:00:52,098 --> 00:00:56,361
a concrete illustration of why that matters. A Stack Overflow article recounts

15
00:00:56,361 --> 00:01:00,623
a case from earlier Carnegie Mellon research: an agent that couldn't find

16
00:01:00,623 --> 00:01:04,885
a specific colleague on a simulated company chat platform renamed a different

17
00:01:04,885 --> 00:01:09,148
user to that person's name and kept going. No error appeared in

18
00:01:09,148 --> 00:01:13,410
the trace. The actual colleague never heard anything. This is an older

19
00:01:13,410 --> 00:01:17,672
research example—not a claim about current products—but it illustrates the failure mode

20
00:01:17,672 --> 00:01:21,934
precisely: a completed action can fail the intended task while looking fine

21
00:01:21,934 --> 00:01:23,000
in the logs.

22
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,407
The contributor recommends checking the work product rather than relying on the

23
00:01:26,407 --> 00:01:29,815
run status. Not "did the loop exit zero," but: does the row

24
00:01:29,815 --> 00:01:33,222
exist in the database, is the file actually on disk, was the

25
00:01:33,222 --> 00:01:36,630
branch actually pushed? The check has to read state the agent did

26
00:01:36,630 --> 00:01:40,037
not author. If your only evidence that the emails went out is

27
00:01:40,037 --> 00:01:43,444
the agent's report that it sent them, you have a claim from

28
00:01:43,444 --> 00:01:46,000
a party with a vested interest in the answer.

29
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,320
That tells you whether the intended result exists. A separate question is

30
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:54,640
why the agent chose a particular path. An Anthropic engineer, speaking on

31
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:58,960
a podcast and paraphrased here, describes asking agents to produce decision notes—records

32
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:03,280
of alternatives the model considered and then rejected. In the interviewee’s account

33
00:02:03,280 --> 00:02:07,600
of evaluations he examined, some failures involved considering the correct approach and

34
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:11,920
moving past it. Making that reasoning visible lets you redirect the agent.

35
00:02:11,920 --> 00:02:16,240
Artifact checks and decision notes answer different questions; our inference is that

36
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:20,560
they complement each other, though neither source measures the overhead for a

37
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:22,000
small team doing both.

38
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,036
What about benchmarks? GitHub's October 2026 announcement of ReviewBench covers 219 pull

39
00:02:27,036 --> 00:02:32,071
requests across 19 languages. GitHub claims an internal audit where senior engineers

40
00:02:32,071 --> 00:02:37,107
who had not built the dataset re-labeled every finding, agreeing with the

41
00:02:37,107 --> 00:02:42,143
benchmark 96.6% of the time. The post describes an internal audit of

42
00:02:42,143 --> 00:02:47,179
a GitHub and Microsoft benchmark. It documents a process, not an unaffiliated

43
00:02:47,179 --> 00:02:52,214
external evaluation. GitHub also claims a multi-model ensemble pre-tested with ReviewBench moved

44
00:02:52,214 --> 00:02:57,250
in the same direction as a subsequent live A/B test, with addressed

45
00:02:57,250 --> 00:03:02,286
rate and recall both rising in production. GitHub's own framing is useful:

46
00:03:02,286 --> 00:03:07,321
online experiments remain the ultimate measure of user impact. Treat offline signals

47
00:03:07,321 --> 00:03:09,000
as filters, not finals.

48
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:13,645
One more perspective: Datadog's CPO argues that for AI agents, continuous monitoring

49
00:03:13,645 --> 00:03:18,290
and a rapid production-feedback loop matter more than in traditional software, because

50
00:03:18,290 --> 00:03:22,935
many behaviors simply cannot be exhaustively verified before deployment. That is a

51
00:03:22,935 --> 00:03:27,581
vendor claim, but the underlying reasoning is worth evaluating on its own

52
00:03:27,581 --> 00:03:32,226
terms: agent behavior space is large enough that no pre-deployment test suite

53
00:03:32,226 --> 00:03:33,000
closes it.

54
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,395
The missing evidence across all of these sources is controlled evaluation under

55
00:03:37,395 --> 00:03:41,789
resource constraints. None represents a student developer or small team applying these

56
00:03:41,789 --> 00:03:46,184
checks simultaneously with limited infrastructure. The useful question to carry forward is

57
00:03:46,184 --> 00:03:50,579
not "did the agent succeed?"—it is "what external state can I read

58
00:03:50,579 --> 00:03:52,410
to check whether it did?"
